</figure>
<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take LongVideoBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, LongTimeScope <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zohar et al. (2025)</a></cite>, MLVU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and TimeScope <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zohar et al. (2025)</a></cite> as representative long-video benchmarks and compare against existing token compression and sparse attention methods. As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D More Results on Long-form Video Benchmarks ‣ VideoNSA: Native Sparse Attention Scales Video Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D More Results on Long-form Video Benchmarks ‣ VideoNSA: Native Sparse Attention Scales Video Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix D More Results on Long-form Video Benchmarks ‣ VideoNSA: Native Sparse Attention Scales Video Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix D More Results on Long-form Video Benchmarks ‣ VideoNSA: Native Sparse Attention Scales Video Understanding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">VideoNSA</span> achieves comparable performance without specialized designs. Moreover, we observe that <span id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">VideoNSA</span> significantly outperforms the baselines on subtasks related to temporal reasoning and on videos of extended length.</p>
</div>
</section>
